<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Minor ischemic stroke, left precentral cortex (I63.412). By the tissue-based definition this was a minor ischemic stroke, not a transient ischemic attack: the symptoms resolved, but the MRI shows an infarct in the hand area of the left motor cortex. A single small cortical infarct is embolic in pattern. The likeliest sources are the heart, particularly atrial fibrillation, given her age and a mildly enlarged left atrium, and less likely the left carotid plaque, which narrows the artery by less than 50%. Until a source is found, this is an embolic stroke of undetermined source. [S]</item><item>Antiplatelet therapy (I63.412). Yes. For a minor noncardioembolic stroke or high-risk TIA, aspirin with clopidogrel started within 24 hours and given for 21 days lowers early recurrence (CHANCE, POINT); hers was started at about 18 hours. Bleeding risk rises if it is continued longer, so after 21 days she should take a single antiplatelet, clopidogrel, unless atrial fibrillation is found. [S]</item><item>Rhythm monitoring and atrial fibrillation (I63.412). An embolic-appearing stroke at 78 warrants at least 30 days of ambulatory monitoring; a 30-day patch was applied today. If atrial fibrillation is found, stop the antiplatelet and start a direct oral anticoagulant: apixaban 5 mg twice daily, since she meets only one dose-reduction criterion (weight 60 kg or less). With a small infarct and no hemorrhage, anticoagulation can begin as soon as atrial fibrillation is confirmed. [S]</item><item>Carotid plaque. Less than 50% narrowing does not warrant endarterectomy or stenting; treatment is a statin, blood pressure control and her antithrombotic. No follow-up carotid imaging is needed unless new symptoms arise. [S]</item><item>Targets and screening (I10). Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg, with treatment started a few days after the stroke; an ARB or thiazide is suitable, at a low starting dose for her size. LDL below 70 mg/dL with atorvastatin 40 mg, adding ezetimibe if needed. She passed the swallow screen; MoCA 26 is normal and should be repeated within the first year; PHQ-9 at the first follow-up; falls assessment before anticoagulation and at each visit; a sleep apnea screen. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Morrison-Takeda&apos;s note and ECG (sinus rhythm). Emergency department: non-contrast CT with no hemorrhage; CT angiography with no large-vessel occlusion or intracranial stenosis and a calcified plaque at the origin of the left internal carotid artery narrowing it by less than 50%; bedside swallow screen passed; hemoglobin 12.8 g/dL, platelets 232, creatinine 0.82 mg/dL, glucose 98 mg/dL, INR 1.0, hemoglobin A1c 5.5%, LDL 132 mg/dL. This morning: MRI with diffusion showing an 8 mm acute infarct in the cortex of the left precentral gyrus and mild small-vessel white matter changes; transthoracic echocardiogram with an ejection fraction of 60%, a mildly enlarged left atrium (37 mL/m2) and no thrombus. MoCA 26. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Aspirin 81 mg and clopidogrel 75 mg daily to complete 21 days, then clopidogrel 75 mg alone. [S]</item><item>30-day ambulatory rhythm monitoring (applied today). [S]</item><item>If atrial fibrillation is found: stop clopidogrel and start apixaban 5 mg twice daily, with a blood count and kidney function first. [S]</item><item>Atorvastatin 40 mg daily; lipid panel in four to twelve weeks; LDL goal below 70 mg/dL. [S]</item><item>Home blood pressure readings; if the average stays 130/80 mmHg or higher after the first week, start losartan 25 mg daily with potassium and creatinine in two weeks; target below 130/80. [S]</item><item>No carotid procedure and no routine follow-up carotid imaging. [S]</item><item>MoCA again within the first year; PHQ-9 and a sleep apnea screen at the three-month review; falls assessment at each visit. [S]</item><item>BE FAST and 911 for any new symptom, even if it passes. [S]</item><item>Her Native Hawaiian healing practices can continue alongside this plan; any bodywork such as lomilomi should use gentle pressure once she takes an anticoagulant. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Morrison-Takeda, who saw her the same day. The MRI and the plan were explained plainly to Liliʻuokalani and to the members of her ʻohana she invited; she asked questions and agreed to the monitor, the antiplatelets and the statin. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Dr. Morrison-Takeda continues her care, with the monitor result at about five weeks. Video review with vascular neurology at three months, or sooner with any new symptom. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
